<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leif Erikson</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 970–c. 1020</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Leifr Eiríksson, Leif the Lucky</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Leif Erikson was a Norse explorer, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erik-the-red">Erik the Red</a>, the founder of the Norse settlements in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a>. According to the Icelandic sagas he sailed from Greenland around 1000 to lands he called Helluland, Markland and Vinland, making him one of the first Europeans to reach <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>. The sagas also credit him with bringing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> to Greenland. The site at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lanse-aux-meadows">L'Anse aux Meadows</a> confirms a Norse presence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leif-erikson.jpg" alt="Leif Erikson" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sculptor Daniels, John K. 1875-1978 Photo: Sharon Mollerus · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leif_Erikson_Statue,_Duluth_(15290644106).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Leif was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erik-the-red">Erik the Red</a> and his wife Thjodhild, and the grandson of Thorvald Ásvaldsson, who was banished from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> for manslaughter. His birthplace is not given in the sagas, but he was probably born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iceland">Iceland</a>, perhaps in the Haukadalur valley, where his mother's family was based and where Erik built the farmstead Eiríksstaðir around 970. Erik was later banished from Iceland and sailed west to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a>, where he founded the first permanent settlement in 986. Leif grew up on the family estate of Brattahlíð in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-settlement">Eastern Settlement</a>. He had two brothers, Thorstein and Thorvald, and a sister, Freydís, and he called a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrall">thrall</a> named Tyrker his foster father.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Two sagas thought to have been written around 1200, the Saga of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erik-the-red">Erik the Red</a> and the Saga of the Greenlanders, give detailed but sometimes conflicting accounts of the voyages to Vinland. They are generally regarded as works of literature, and scholars debate how far they are accurate. In the first, Leif was blown off course on the way from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a>, having been converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> at the court of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaf-tryggvason">Olaf Tryggvason</a>, and found wild grapes, self-sown wheat and maple trees.</p><p>In the second, he bought the ship of Bjarni Herjólfsson, who had earlier sighted unknown coasts, and sailed with thirty-five men to Helluland, Markland and then a fertile land he named Vinland, where his party built a settlement called Leifsbudir. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a> with a cargo of grapes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, and he never went back to Vinland.</p><p>The site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lanse-aux-meadows">L'Anse aux Meadows</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a>, found in the early 1960s by Helge Ingstad and Anne Stine Ingstad, has been suggested as Leifsbudir. Carbon dating puts it at 990 to 1050, and tree-ring analysis at 1021. Later evidence suggests Vinland may have been the area around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-st-lawrence">Gulf of St. Lawrence</a>, with L'Anse aux Meadows a ship repair station and waypoint.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Erik died shortly after 1000, and Leif became paramount chief in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a>. He is last mentioned alive in 1018, in the Saga of St. Olaf, and by 1025 his son Thorkell held the chieftaincy; the sagas do not tell of his death. Leif is in all likelihood a historical figure, but other details of his life are debated, and some scholars think Freydís and Tyrker are fictional, as may be his missionary commission. His expedition encouraged other Norsemen to follow, though no Norse settlement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> was permanent.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Leif Erikson” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son <b>Leif Erikson</b> sailed from Greenland to North America around 1000 and is credited with bringing Christianity.</blockquote><a href="/state/norse-greenland">Norse Greenland</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Eiríksstaðir, in what is now <a href="/countries/iceland">Iceland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=65.06,-21.54&amp;year=1002" aria-label="Eiríksstaðir on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Leif Erikson"><image href="/maps/globes/g330n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 4548h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 4548h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=65.06,-21.54&amp;year=1002">What was here in 1002? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-family"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Family</h2><ul class="enc-seealso enc-fam"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/erik-the-red"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/erik-the-red.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Erik the Red</span><span class="tp">Father &#183; c. 950–c. 1003</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="ft-note">Parents, spouses and children as Wikidata records them, among the people of the encyclopedia: not the whole family.</p></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;We have not failed to land, like Bjarni; now I will give this country a name, and call it Helluland (the land of flat stone).&#8221;</p><footer>Tale of the Greenlanders (Grænlendinga saga) &#183; tr. G. M. Gathorne-Hardy, 1921 &#183; the saga's account, written down about two centuries later; on the first shore he reached west of Greenland &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/76383/pg76383-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
